614 1069 "q-337108" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445614 1071 "Consider the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. You're given that the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ is the unique solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n}.\n$$\n\nA derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x({t}_{0})$ is presented below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}\\,c$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $x({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}x({t}_{0})=({e}^{A{t}_{0}})^{-1}{e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})\\,$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that {{field-3}}." 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445614 1072 #uuid "c3c2f127-8fe3-52c5-8ea9-fa818612f56c" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445614 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L2},$ and $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2},$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\text{L1}$, we substitute ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ into this equation to obtain\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L3}.$ Thus, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{substituting }{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}\\text{ into }x(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L7}.$ Using line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}\\,c.\n$$\n\nFrom line $\\text{L6}$, we know that $c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}).$ Substituting this expression for $c$ into the formula for $x(t)$ gives\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At}{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L7}.$ Thus, line $\\text{L7}$ can be obtained by $\\text{substituting }c={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0})\\text{ into }x(t)={e}^{At}c.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L8}$ and $\\text{L9}.$ Starting from $\\text{L8},$\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{At-A{t}_{0}}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwe rewrite the exponent using the algebraic identity $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})$ to get\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhich is line $\\text{L9}.$ Thus, $\\text{L9}$ follows from $\\text{L8}$ by the fact that $At-A{t}_{0}=A(t-{t}_{0})\\text{ for any }t\\text{ and }{t}_{0}.$" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445604 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445607 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1069 "q-335427" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(3) \\\\ y(3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1072 #uuid "e674cbe1-254a-5d93-a9b4-be22a2029aaf" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true] [17592186445615 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-3)}\\,x(3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-3)} & (t-3){e}^{4(t-3)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-3)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-{e}^{4(t-3)}\\, \\\\ \\,0\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534955 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0344a228-63e9-598d-8026-7a699136dec3" 17592186445537 "answer-0647c748-1350-542c-8a31-93d7ececc1bc" 17592186445538 "answer-0a087c4f-5999-5d51-b329-5bde4e70b5a8" 17592186445539 "answer-0c29a2cd-62e5-5fd1-b667-d19225a99baf" 17592186445540 "answer-0d7bb90c-0a41-5654-826b-4c1b3874378f" 17592186445541 "answer-12579